 or the host\'s ceiling, governs. All sizes are ciphertext bytes.","properties":{"maxFiles":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"The number of committed blobs the scope may hold. 0 refuses every upload at this scope."},"maxFileBytes":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":1073741824,"description":"The largest single blob the scope accepts. Never above 1 GiB, the transfer ceiling (BlobManifest)."},"maxBytes":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"The total size of the committed and reserved blobs the scope may hold."}}},"Usage":{"title":"Usage","type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["files","bytes","reservedBytes"],"description":"What one scope holds now. Ciphertext bytes throughout.","properties":{"files":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Committed blobs that are not orphaned."},"bytes":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Their total size. A blob stops counting the moment it is orphaned (its last record dropped it or was retracted), not when the host finally deletes it, so a member who deletes a file has the room back at once."},"reservedBytes":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Bytes reserved by uploads begun and not yet committed, aborted or expired. Counted against `maxBytes`, so two concurrent uploads cannot both fit under a limit only one of them fits."},"reservedFiles":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Uploads begun and not yet committed, aborted or expired. Counted against `maxFiles`."}}},"RoomLimits":{"title":"RoomLimits","type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["filesEnabled"],"description":"How much a host is willing to store for one room, at the room and per member. A hosting decision, set by the host and never by anything in the room\'s credentials. An upload must fit every scope at once: the object against the smallest `maxFileBytes` that applies, and the member and the room with the file added.","properties":{"filesEnabled":{"type":"boolean","description":"False refuses every rooms/blobs/* upload for the room. Reads of blobs already stored continue."},"room":{"\$ref":"#/\$defs/ScopeLimits","description":"Limits on the room as a whole."},"member":{"\$ref":"#/\$defs/ScopeLimits","description":"Limits on each member\'s own uploads in this room, unless that member has an override. A member is the subject at the root of the authority chain they present, never the key that signed, so an agent acting on an attenuated chain spends its member\'s allowance. Not applied on a `private` room, where the host cannot tell members apart; the room and object limits still hold there."}}},"RecordPrune":{"title":"RecordPrune","type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["version","at","removed"],"description":"The mark a prune leaves: **that** records were removed, when, and how many — never which, and never anything they said.\\n\\nA prune removes records outright, tombstones included, so the room\'s record tree loses leaves and an incremental reader has nothing left to learn the removal from. This is what it learns from instead. A host keeps the latest one per room and serves it with every listing that can carry it (`rooms/records/list` 0.3, as `lastPrune`); a reader whose own copy of the room was synchronised to a version below `version` MUST rebuild that copy from a full listing — no `sinceVersion` — and drop every record the full listing no longer holds. Without that, a record the room removed lives on in every cache that synchronised before the prune, and a record retracted while the reader was away comes back as current.\\n\\n**What a host keeps and what it does not.** A host keeps this marker, and in its audit trail the prune\'s `reason`, its requester where the tier discloses one, and the retention it was made under. It MUST NOT keep anything a pruned record said — body, ciphertext, cleartext, title, description, file or blob — and SHOULD NOT keep pruned records\' keys: the count is the audit, and a list of keys on an `open` room, where keys may be descriptive, is content by another name.","properties":{"version":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"The version the prune took from the room\'s counter, as a put or a curation would. No record carries it; it is what moves `HeadVersion` past the removal, so that a root taken before and a root taken after are never labelled with one version."},"at":{"type":"string","format":"date-time","description":"When the host performed it."},"removed":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"How many records were removed, tombstones included. A prune that removed nothing leaves no mark."}}},"RecordRetention":{"title":"RecordRetention","type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"description":"How long a room keeps its records, by age: the room\'s **record retention**. Records older than it states become **eligible** to be removed by `rooms/records/prune`; nothing is removed until a holder of `admin` asks for a prune, and nothing a prune removes can be brought back.\\n\\n**Not `retentionPolicy`, and not `retentionDays`.** Both names are taken in this family and mean other things. `retentionPolicy` (`RetentionPolicy`) is whether a room\'s *key chain* lets a member read epochs from before they joined, fixed at creation because key material either exists for an epoch or it does not. `retentionDays` (`rooms/create`) is how long a host keeps a whole room after it lapses unrenewed. This is a third question — how long the room keeps its *records* while it is live — and unlike the first it is a decision a room revisits, so it is changeable (`rooms/records/retention/set`). A name sharing either\'s would invite a reader to apply one\'s rules to the other.\\n\\n**Age is the host\'s to measure, so age is `updatedAt`.** On the sealed tiers a host can read nothing of a record but its key, version, status, pinning and when it last changed, so a policy the host applies can only be stated in those terms. That is also why it cannot use a record\'s own `validUntil`, which is sealed: expiry is a reader\'s rule, retention a host\'s. A curation is a change, so it restarts a record\'s age — a retraction starts a tombstone\'s.\\n\\n**Absent, or `{}`, means no record is ever eligible by age**, and that is the reading a host MUST apply whenever it cannot read a policy: removing data under a rule nobody stated is the failure this member exists to prevent. Every member can read the policy in force (`rooms/info`), so a record is never removed under a rule its readers could not have known.","properties":{"tombstoneDays":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":36500,"description":"Days a retracted record\'s tombstone is kept after its retraction. A tombstone whose `updatedAt` is more than this long ago is eligible. Tombstones exist so that a member syncing incrementally learns of a retraction (`rooms/records/curate`); this is how long a member may stay away and still learn of one that way rather than by rebuilding their copy of the room. Absent means tombstones are never eligible."},"recordDays":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":36500,"description":"Days a record is kept after it last changed, whatever its standing. A record — `active`, `deprecated` or `retracted` — whose `updatedAt` is more than this long ago is eligible, **unless it is pinned**: pinning says what matters in the room, and a room that wants a pinned record gone unpins it first. Absent means records are never eligible by age."},"ext":{"\$ref":"#/\$defs/Ext","description":"Ecosystem-defined extension members per SPEC.md §4.5.1. A host MUST NOT treat a member here as making more records eligible: a removal rule a member\'s reader cannot understand is one they were never told about."}}},"RetentionPolicy":{"title":"RetentionPolicy","type":"string","enum":["chained","fromJoin"],"description":"Whether a room keeps its history readable across a membership change, fixed at creation and immutable thereafter — like `Visibility`, and for the same reason: the rungs of an epoch key chain either exist for an epoch or they do not, and no later change of mind can seal key material that was never sealed or unseal what was already severed. `chained`: each advance produces an `EpochLink`, so every member reads the room\'s whole retained history however long they have been in it — what a **library** wants, at the cost of post-compromise security for record content, since a compromised current key then reaches every retained epoch. `fromJoin`: no rungs are produced, so a member reads only from the epoch their group state is at — what a **stream** wants, and what a room under a strict forward-secrecy obligation wants, at the cost that a joining member finds an empty-looking room and nobody can reread a record once their group state has moved past the epoch it was sealed under. Absent means `chained`; see the prose on why the absent case is the readable one.","\$comment":"Deliberately no JSON Schema `default`. A declared default is materialised by the generated bindings — the member becomes non-optional with a serde default — so an absent value would reappear as an explicit one on re-serialisation and break round-trip idempotence for every document written before this member existed. The meaning of absence is stated in prose, where a binding cannot act on it."},"Visibility":{"title":"Visibility","type":"string","enum":["open","attributed","private"],"description":"How much of a room its host can see, fixed at creation and immutable thereafter. `open`: records are cleartext, searchable and fully audited. `attributed`: record content is sealed, and the host still learns which member acted. `private`: content is sealed and membership is presented in zero knowledge, so the host verifies that a member acted without learning which. Immutable because a downgrade cannot un-see cleartext and an upgrade would protect only what came after while presenting as though it protected everything."},"AuthorityPresentation":{"title":"AuthorityPresentation","type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["membership","authority"],"description":"What a party presents to act on a room. Carries the whole authority chain: a host MUST NOT dereference an authority credential\'s `parent` to fetch a link it was not given. Resolving over the network would make verification depend on availability, turn every identifier into a request the host can be induced to make against an address the holder chooses, and signal credential use to whoever hosts the identifier. A host MUST bind the presenter to the chain\'s leaf. A chain that verifies is evidence that authority was conferred on somebody; it is not evidence that the party presenting it is that somebody. The leaf\'s subject MUST equal the party the host authenticated for this request — an identity the transport established or a document `proof` proved, never one named in a payload. A host that omits this check authorizes every captured presentation, and the omission is silent, because the chain still verifies.\\n\\n**Every credential is its issuer\'s attestation, and only its issuer\'s.** For the membership credential and for every link of `authority`, a host MUST verify the credential\'s `proof` with a key that the credential\'s own `issuer` lists for `assertionMethod` in its DID document, and MUST refuse a credential whose proof names a verification method that is not its issuer\'s. A proof that verifies under some other party\'s key is that party\'s statement, not the issuer\'s, and confers nothing. The same binding holds for every other document a room signs, such as a `RevocationList`.\\n\\n**A chain that verifies may still be revoked.** A host that has accepted a `RevocationList` for the room (`rooms/revocations/put`) MUST, from the moment it accepted it, refuse every presentation in which the membership credential or any link of `authority` is revoked by that list, under every task and every version of every task it serves for the room. A chain from which any link is revoked is refused whole: a credential delegated from a revoked one confers nothing, however it was attenuated. The refusal is the same `permissionDenied` as any other failed presentation.","properties":{"membership":{"type":"string","description":"The presenter\'s membership credential for this room, or — on a `private` room — a zero-knowledge presentation of it. Serialized per the governing profile."},"authority":{"type":"array","minItems":1,"maxItems":8,"items":{"type":"string"},"description":"The authority chain, LEAF FIRST: the first element is the credential being relied on and the last MUST be one issued by the room itself. Every link the presenter relies on is present, because the host will not fetch one. Capped at 8: verification is linear in chain length and runs on every operation, so an unbounded chain is a denial-of-service surface against the host. The known uses need 2 to 3 — a person attenuating to an agent, and that agent to a sub-agent."},"subjectBinding":{"type":"string","description":"REQUIRED on a `private` room, where the subject identifier is withheld: a proof that the membership credential and the authority chain\'s leaf describe the SAME subject. Without it two parties pool credentials — one contributes membership, the other authority — and the combination verifies as a single party holding both. A host MUST refuse a private-room presentation that omits this."}}}}}';

